libipa: sensor helper for the Hi-1337, the ultra-wide of the Xiaomi POCO X3 NFC

THE PROBLEM
-----------
Without a helper, libcamera warns

    IPASoft: Failed to create camera sensor helper for hi1337

and its automatic control cannot reason about gain: it is left with exposure
alone. Measured on the phone, in low light the AGC raises the exposure ALL THE
WAY TO THE TOP and leaves the gain at its unity value:

    before:   exposure: 720   analogue_gain: 512
    after:    exposure: 1714  analogue_gain: 512

With the exposure at its maximum the frame is stretched so that it fits, so the
camera crawls instead of raising the gain. That is what shows up as "the
ultra-wide is slow in low light".

WHERE THE CONSTANTS COME FROM
-----------------------------
From measuring them on the sensor, not from a datasheet I do not have.

The gain, with the exposure FIXED and the scene still, looking at the signal
above the pedestal AND at the contrast -which is what tells a gain apart from a
shift of the black level-:

    code    signal  contrast
       512   9.58        25.0
      1024  19.57        49.0    (x2.04 and x1.96)
      2048  39.49        96.0    (x4.12 and x3.84)

Both quantities rise in proportion to the code, that is, the curve is a straight
line through the origin with unity at 512: gain = code / 512.

The black level, with the scene in the dark: every pixel lands at 16 of the
eight high bits, with no spread. They are the usual 64 of ten bits, which on the
helper's 16-bit scale is 4096.

A WARNING FOR WHOEVER TOUCHES THIS SENSOR
-----------------------------------------
The gain is FOUR registers, one per Bayer channel -0x0214, 0x0216, 0x0218 and
0x021a-, which have to be written together. The 0x0204 sitting next to them is
NOT gain even though it looks like it: it is the black level. They can be told
apart because raising it makes the mean grow but the contrast GO DOWN, whereas a
gain raises both. Confusing them cost three wrong conclusions in a row.

